WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:01.913
- Welcome back, y'all.

00:00:01.913 --> 00:00:04.830
Our next guest is a New York
Times bestselling author

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.690
with more than a dozen books to his name,

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.990
including "When I was the
Greatest," "All American Boys,"

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:13.950
and "Long Way Down," "Stamped:Racism,
Antiracism, and You."

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.260
Now he's debuting his
very first children's book

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.570
called, "There Was a Party for Langston."

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.325
Please everybody welcome Jason Reynolds.

00:00:21.325 --> 00:00:23.051
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:00:23.051 --> 00:00:25.718
(upbeat music)

00:00:36.594 --> 00:00:37.773
All right.

00:00:37.773 --> 00:00:38.606
I have to lead with this

00:00:38.606 --> 00:00:41.010
'cause I mean, novels, poetry, nonfiction,

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.690
this is usually your field, right?

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:43.570
- Yes.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:44.553
- This is new for you,
picture books, right?

00:00:44.553 --> 00:00:46.380
- Yes, yes, yes, very new for me.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.030
- What made you wanna do it?

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:48.930
- Well, I mean, first of all,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.400
because there are
children that age, right?

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.370
I specialize primarily in young people.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.580
And that goes from 18, 19 all the way

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.260
now to the youngest of the lot.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.093
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:59.093 --> 00:00:59.926
- Which are the babies.

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:00.759
And it's been amazing for me.

00:01:00.759 --> 00:01:02.190
- I know, it's a different way, right?

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.023
What's the process?

00:01:03.023 --> 00:01:03.870
How is it different from?

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:05.010
- It's an incredible challenge.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.261
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:06.261 --> 00:01:07.094
- You've done this right?

00:01:07.094 --> 00:01:08.847
- I honestly, when I was
like, this would be easy.

00:01:08.847 --> 00:01:09.930
- This is.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:10.927
- Oh my God.

00:01:10.927 --> 00:01:12.600
- Yeah, this is what every
everybody is like, oh, nah man,

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:13.620
this is like the easiest.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.210
All you gotta, it's like 50 words.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.850
And I'm like, yeah, it's like 50 words.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:19.740
- Oh my God, it's harder.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:20.573
- It's much more different.

00:01:20.573 --> 00:01:22.980
- And to keep them captivated,
to write something like that.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.180
- And you don't always know

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.520
what the thing will be that
will keep them captivated.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.500
I think with novels there's narrative arc.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.170
There's all kind of structure
and character analyses

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:32.880
and all this other
stuff you can do, right?

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:33.930
- Yeah.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:34.770
- But with this, you get just a few words

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.530
to try to figure out how
to hit a particular pulse

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:38.730
with a population of people

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.130
that you don't always quite understand.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:42.570
- [Kelly] Absolutely, yeah.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.403
- Right, and so it's complicated.

00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:45.471
- Yeah, so why Langston Hughes?

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:47.040
- Why Langston Hughes?

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.710
You know, to me, Langston Hughes

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.388
is one of the most important
literary luminaries.

00:01:53.388 --> 00:01:56.700
I think that he birthed
multiple generations of writers,

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.590
and in this case,
specifically Black writers.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.630
A lot of us came up studying the works

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.780
of a man whose work
seemed to be at eye level,

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.470
though truthfully was
far more sophisticated

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.300
than I think we give credit to.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.190
It's the idea that there
is a vast difference

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:15.690
between that which is easy
and that which is simple.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.418
Right, just because
the thing reads as easy

00:02:17.418 --> 00:02:19.590
does not mean that it was simply made.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.030
It usually means the opposite.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.244
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:22.244 --> 00:02:23.242
- Right?

00:02:23.242 --> 00:02:24.075
That it was very difficult to make a thing

00:02:24.075 --> 00:02:26.160
that feels and reads and sounds easy.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.530
And Langston Hughes
was the master of that.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.510
- Yeah, to make it look
easy, and it's not.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.020
Oh my gosh.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.023
It's like a magician.

00:02:33.023 --> 00:02:34.020
- You know this, absolutely.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.023
- Yeah, I'm like, yeah.

00:02:35.023 --> 00:02:37.284
Well, so the book was inspired
by this picture, right?

00:02:37.284 --> 00:02:38.700
- [Jason] Yes, yes.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.143
- [Kelly] I love this picture.

00:02:40.143 --> 00:02:41.140
- [Jason] It's amazing.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:42.143
- [Kelly] So much joy.

00:02:42.143 --> 00:02:43.076
- [Jason] So much joy.

00:02:43.076 --> 00:02:43.909
- So much pride,

00:02:43.909 --> 00:02:44.742
so much going on in this.

00:02:44.742 --> 00:02:45.583
Why this picture for you?

00:02:45.583 --> 00:02:47.790
- You know, I mean,
first of all, the picture

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:51.180
in and of itself is amazing
to see these two icons.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.800
Maya Angelou, Amiri Baraka.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.820
So we all know who Maya
Angelou was and is.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.979
- [Kelly] Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:56.979 --> 00:02:58.303
(audience applauding)

00:02:58.303 --> 00:03:01.620
- And Amiri Baraka was one of the leaders

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.640
of the Black arts movement,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.410
a very important figure in poetry,

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.720
specifically through 1960s and 70s.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.070
But for a lot of us coming up,

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:11.250
he was sort of like a OG figure,

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.230
somebody that we could look to as a hero.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.020
So to see this photograph
and to see our two heroes,

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:17.760
two of our heroes dancing,

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:18.870
and to know that they were dancing

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:20.910
at the Schaumburg Library right uptown.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:21.750
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.790
- And they were dancing on a cosmogram

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.530
on the floor in the Schaumburg.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.722
And underneath that
floor, that piece of floor

00:03:27.722 --> 00:03:32.070
were and are the ashes of Langston Hughes.

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:33.300
They're buried under that floor.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.680
So when we talk about,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.760
when we talk about standing
on the shoulders of giants.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.593
- Yeah.

00:03:39.593 --> 00:03:41.310
- We're talking about
people literally doing so.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.500
And I would argue they're
doing it better than most

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:47.100
because they're choosing not
just to stand, but to dance.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:47.933
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:47.933 --> 00:03:48.930
- Right, and so there's something to that

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:50.194
that we should all consider.

00:03:50.194 --> 00:03:51.027
- And to continue to inspire.

00:03:51.027 --> 00:03:51.860
- Absolutely.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:52.693
What's the point of standing,

00:03:52.693 --> 00:03:53.526
if you're not going do something.

00:03:53.526 --> 00:03:54.980
Make your move, right?

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:55.980
- [Kelly] Absolutely.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:56.813
Yeah, oh my gosh.

00:03:56.813 --> 00:03:58.500
You are great with words, my friend.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.120
Yes, oh my gosh.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:01.110
And your tone.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:02.460
I'm like, okay.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.380
Jason's newest book, I'm sold.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:05.857
Jason's newest book is called,

00:04:05.857 --> 00:04:07.470
"There was a Party for Langston,"

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.179
and everyone in the audience
is going home with a copy.

00:04:10.179 --> 00:04:11.012
(audience cheering and applauding)

